  <h2 id="cost">What each path really costs, and how to protect your education budget</h2>

  <p>
    Nursing, physiotherapy, pharmacy, paramedical courses, public health, and biomedical engineering are mandatory professional qualifications, not optional skill add-ons — so the usual "spend no more than a small slice of your budget on one course" rule does not apply to the base degree itself in the same way it would to a discretionary weekend course. What it should still shape is which college you pick within that degree.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Track</th>
          <th>Government college (approx. total)</th>
          <th>Private college (approx. total)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {costRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Track"><strong>{item.track}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Government college (approx. total)">{item.government}</td>
            <td data-label="Private college (approx. total)">{item.private}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    As a strict planning heuristic (not a universal rule), if you are choosing a private college that costs several times more than the government-college range above, that extra spend should be justified by something specific and checkable — NCAHP or Nursing Council accreditation status, current faculty and lab quality, real clinical or hospital tie-ups, verified placement outcomes for the last two to three batches, and alumni you can actually talk to. "It has a bigger campus" or "it is well known in my city" is not evidence.
  </p>

  <p>
    The add-on certifications mentioned throughout this article — CPC/CCS for coding, GCP for clinical research, MPT for physiotherapy specialisation — are a different category of spend. They usually cost a few thousand to a few tens of thousands of rupees against a starting salary of Rs 2.5-4.5 LPA, which is why they are low-risk, high-leverage additions worth prioritising once the base degree is settled, rather than something to skip to save a small amount.
  </p>

  <h2 id="self-learn">Learning and certifying without overspending (for the desk-based tracks)</h2>

  <p>
    This section applies specifically to medical coding, clinical research, public health, and biomedical engineering — the four tracks that do not require a clinical licence. For nursing, physiotherapy, and pharmacy, the formal degree and supervised clinical training are non-negotiable; skip to the next section if that is your track.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Check free and official sources first</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Official NCAHP, CDSCO, and CTRI (Clinical Trials Registry - India) portals for terminology, process documents, and real trial data.</li>
        <li>AAPC and AHIMA publish free introductory material and sample questions before you pay for a full coding course.</li>
        <li>ICH-GCP awareness modules are available free or at low cost through recognised public-health and research-training platforms.</li>
        <li>PHFI and IIPH publish open resources and webinars relevant to public health careers.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What you are actually paying for in a paid course</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A structured syllabus that matches the real CPC, CCS, or GCP exam pattern, instead of scattered free content.</li>
        <li>Practice questions, mock exams, and feedback on your mistakes.</li>
        <li>An exam voucher or proctored exam access bundled into the fee.</li>
        <li>A recognised credential that a recruiter can verify, not just a completion certificate.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Before paying for any specific course, check who teaches it, how recently it was updated, whether it includes practice exams and real feedback, its refund policy, and independent reviews from people who actually cleared the exam afterwards — judge the individual course, not the platform's brand name. Finishing a course is not the same as being job-ready: the proof tasks in the next section are what actually demonstrate the learning to a recruiter.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choose">How to choose by work style, not by how "medical" the name sounds</h2>

  <p>
    The label on the course does not tell you what your actual weekday will feel like.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use work style as the real filter instead.
